e write our tool function with explicit type hints. Second, we configure the workspace settings JSON file. Third, we run our static analyzer to catch configuration defects. Finally, we launch the workspace and verify tool registration in logs.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 8}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Error Handling and Fault Isolation", "bullets": ["Tool execution errors must return formatted failure payloads, not process crashes.", "Timeout boundaries prevent long-running commands from freezing agent sessions.", "Standard protocol error codes distinguish JSON parsing failures from tool logic faults.", "Structured logging streams aid in troubleshooting parameter mismatch errors."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Fault isolation is critical. If a custom tool encounters an uncaught exception, it should return a structured error response rather than crashing the MCP server process. Unhandled process crashes disconnect the agent from all tools provided by that server.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "example", "title": "Integrating Static Analyzers via MCP Protocol", "bullets": ["Agent dispatches code analysis request containing relative file paths.", "Custom MCP server runs static checker and transforms output into standard JSON.", "Agent receives structured line-by-line diagnostic feedback.", "Enables auto-correction loops where agent fixes flagged static code issues."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "MCP Tool Call Response Payload", "steps": [], "code": "{
