{"lecture_id": "W03.S2", "title": "Custom tool integrations using model context protocols", "slides": [{"kind": "concept", "title": "Recapping Context Engineering and Introducing Model Context Protocol", "bullets": ["Last week focused on context engineering and prompt management for coding agents.", "Standard APIs force developers to write custom glue code for each external tool.", "Model Context Protocol provides a standardized protocol for context and tool integration.", "MCP enables AI models to safely read data and invoke external capabilities."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "MCP Interaction Flow", "steps": ["User / Agent Workspace", "MCP Client", "MCP Protocol (JSON-RPC)", "MCP Server (Tools / Data)"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Welcome back. In Week 2, we discussed how coding agents consume context from files and prompts. However, connecting agents to external databases, custom build tools, or internal APIs usually requires ad-hoc integration code. Today, we introduce the Model Context Protocol, an open standard designed to unify how AI applications connect to external tools and context providers. We will look at how MCP structures interactions, how to configure servers in agent workspaces, and how to verify integrations using automated tests and static analysis tools without generating configuration warnings.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "MCP Architecture: Hosts, Clients, and Servers", "bullets": ["MCP Host: The environment where the agent operates.", "MCP Client: Maintains 1:1 connections with one or more MCP servers.", "MCP Server: Exposes specific capabilities like tools, resources, and prompt templates.", "Communication occurs over standard transports like stdio or HTTP with SSE."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "MCP Core Roles Overview", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Role", "Responsibility", "Example"], ["Host", "Runs agent UI and LLM engine", "VS Code / Workspace"], ["Client", "Manages server lifecycle & protocol", "Workspace Plugin"], ["Server", "Exposes custom tools & context", "DB Inspector / Static Analyzer"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Understanding the separation of concerns in MCP architecture is vital before writing custom configurations. The MCP Host is the overarching application, like an IDE. Inside the host, the MCP Client initiates standard connections to individual MCP Servers. Each server operates as an isolated process or service exposing specific tools or context. Because communication uses standard protocols like JSON-RPC over stdio or SSE, servers can be implemented in standard languages like TypeScript or Python while keeping host integration completely decoupled.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "MCP Core Primitives: Resources, Prompts, and Tools", "bullets": ["Resources: Data items readable by the client like files or logs.", "Prompts: Reusable prompt templates with parameters exposed to users and agents.", "Tools: Executable functions that the model can invoke to perform side effects.", "Tools use JSON Schema to define expected parameters and return types."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "MCP exposes three primary primitives. Resources represent passive context that the model or client can read, such as log files or static documentation. Prompts are predefined templates that help structure specific workflows. Tools are active, executable functions—such as running a build script, running a static analysis tool, or querying a database. When defining a custom tool integration, you must supply precise JSON Schemas for tool inputs so the AI agent knows how to construct valid calls.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Configuring MCP Servers in Agent Workspaces", "bullets": ["Agent workspaces define server configurations in standard JSON configuration files.", "Configuration specifies server executable command, arguments, and environment variables.", "Configuration files must strictly match the workspace's expected JSON schema.", "Syntax errors or invalid properties trigger static analyzer configuration warnings."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "To integrate a custom tool into an agent workspace, you configure an MCP client JSON file. This file tells the client how to launch or connect to your MCP server process. Typical configuration settings include the executable binary name, command-line arguments, environment variables, and feature flags. If your configuration includes unsupported properties or syntax errors, static analysis or schema validation in the workspace will generate warnings or fail outright, preventing tool discovery.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "example", "title": "Example Workspace MCP Configuration", "bullets": ["Declares external MCP server launched via local stdio process.", "Specifies command, path to server script, and required environment variables.", "Passes static validation without schema warnings."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Valid MCP Client Configuration JSON", "steps": [], "code": "{
  "mcpServers": {
    "db-tool": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["./build/index.js"],
      "env": {
        "DB_URL": "sqlite:///data/app.db",
        "LOG_LEVEL": "info"
      }
    }
  }
}", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Here is a concrete JSON configuration for registering a custom database inspection MCP server within an agent workspace configuration. Notice the mcpServers object mapping a server identifier to its execution command and arguments. When the workspace loads, it validates this structure against the official schema. If key names are misspelled or invalid fields are introduced, static analyzers flag errors. Following standard structure ensures clean startup without warnings and allows the agent to discover tools cleanly.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Tool Schema Definitions and Input Validation", "bullets": ["Tools require explicit input schemas written in JSON Schema format.", "Agents inspect parameters, required fields, and types before generating calls.", "Strict validation prevents invalid model outputs from reaching server logic.", "Runtime validation libraries like Zod ensure full type safety in code."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "When writing a tool handler in your MCP server, defining an explicit schema is critical. The host supplies this JSON schema to the AI model so the model understands required parameters, parameter types, and descriptions. Inside your server implementation, you should use runtime schema validation, like Zod in TypeScript, to ensure incoming calls match expected types. Strict validation stops bad arguments early and ensures deterministic responses to the client.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "Implementing a Custom MCP Tool Server", "bullets": ["Task: Implement a custom MCP server exposing a run_linter tool.", "Define input schema with source file target and fix flags.", "Register tool call handler using the official MCP SDK.", "Ensure no static analyzer warnings on server implementation."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "TypeScript MCP Server Tool Registration", "steps": [], "code": "import { Server } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/index.js';
import { CallToolRequestSchema, ListToolsRequestSchema } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/types.js';

const server = new Server({ name: 'linter-server', version: '1.0.0' }, { capabilities: { tools: {} } });
server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => ({
  tools: [{ name: 'run_linter', description: 'Run code linter', inputSchema: { type: 'object', properties: { path: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['path'] } }]
}));
server.setRequestHandler(CallToolRequestSchema, async (req) => {
  if (req.params.name !== 'run_linter') throw new Error('Tool not found');
  return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: 'Linting passed with 0 errors.' }] };
});", "language": "typescript", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Let's work through writing a minimal custom MCP server in TypeScript. We create a Server instance, declare tool capabilities, and register handlers for ListToolsRequestSchema and CallToolRequestSchema. Notice how the tool definition specifies name, description, and input Schema. When the agent queries available tools, the server responds with this schema. When the agent invokes run_linter, the server validates the input and returns structured content. Writing clean TypeScript with strict types ensures zero static analysis warnings.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 9}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Concept Check: MCP Server Integration", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "In the Model Context Protocol (MCP) architecture, which component is responsible for defining executable tools and handling tool call requests?", "options": ["MCP Host", "MCP Client", "MCP Server", "Static Analyzer"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Correct! The MCP Server defines capabilities like tools and resources and handles call requests. The Host runs the overall application/IDE, and the Client manages server connections."}, "notes": "Take a moment to answer this question. Remember the responsibilities of each architectural component in MCP. The Server exposes tools and handles execution requests; the Client manages communication connections; and the Host provides the top-level user workspace.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 4}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Error Handling and Static Analysis Rules for Integration", "bullets": ["Static analyzers inspect JSON configs and TypeScript code for schema errors.", "Unused imports, missing environment variables, or schema mismatches trigger warnings.", "MCP tool responses must return structured errors rather than unhandled server crashes.", "Passing static checks and integration tests is required for workspace deployment."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Validation Pipeline for Custom MCP Integration", "steps": ["Config & Code Changes", "Static Analyzer Check", "Unit / Integration Test Suite", "Deployment to Workspace"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "When integrating custom MCP servers, quality assurance requires two levels of verification: static analysis and integration testing. Static analysis verifies that configuration JSON files adhere to strict schema rules and that server source code has no type errors or unused variables. Automated integration tests verify that tool calls execute correctly and return structured response payloads. Unhandled exceptions in tool handlers cause protocol errors, so errors must be caught and returned gracefully as content objects.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "Fixing Configuration and Analysis Warnings", "bullets": ["Scenario: Static analyzer reports unknown key env_vars in config file.", "Problem: Server fails integration test due to missing error handling.", "Step 1: Fix configuration key to standard env field.", "Step 2: Wrap server tool logic in try-catch to return error response."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Correcting Config Schema and Error Handling", "steps": [], "code": "// Before Fix in mcp_config.json:
// "env_vars": { "TOKEN": "123" } -> Static Analyzer Warning!

// Corrected mcp_config.json:
// "env": { "TOKEN": "123" }

// Implementation Error Handling Fix:
try {
  const result = await executeTool(req.params.arguments);
  return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: result }] };
} catch (err) {
  return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: `Error: ${err.message}` }], isError: true };
}", "language": "javascript", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Let's walk through troubleshooting a common configuration and static analysis failure. Here, a developer used env_vars instead of env in the configuration file, causing a static analysis schema warning. Simultaneously, the tool handler threw uncaught exceptions when given invalid paths, failing integration tests. We resolve the schema error by changing env_vars to env, and we update the handler to catch errors and return isError: true in the MCP content payload. Now all static analyzer warnings and integration tests pass.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 9}, {"kind": "activity", "title": "In-Class Activity: Custom MCP Tool Integration", "bullets": ["Task: Configure and test a custom MCP tool server on your machine.", "Step 1: Inspect mcp_config.json and fix any static analyzer warnings.", "Step 2: Implement input validation in tool server for run_tests.", "Step 3: Run npm test and verify zero compiler or linter warnings.", "Deliverable: Passing test output and clean static analysis report."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Now it is time for hands-on practice. Open your lab repository for Week 3. You will find a workspace with an unconfigured MCP server and a configuration file containing schema issues. Your goal is to fix the JSON config, complete the tool handler implementation with valid schema validation, and ensure all unit and integration tests pass with zero warnings from the static analyzer. Work with your partner and raise your hand if you hit standard transport issues.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 12}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Concept Check: Verification and Error Handling", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "How should an MCP server tool handler respond when an internal tool execution encounters an expected runtime error (e.g. file not found)?", "options": ["Throw an unhandled JavaScript exception to crash the stdio process", "Return a response object containing error text and 'isError: true'", "Return null and terminate the stdio connection immediately", "Silently ignore the error and return an empty success string"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Correct! MCP tools should return structured error responses using 'isError: true' and descriptive text. Crashing the process or returning invalid payloads breaks client protocol communication and fails integration tests."}, "notes": "Let's check your understanding of error propagation in MCP servers. Crashing the stdio process disrupts the entire MCP client connection. Returning a structured response with isError: true allows the client and agent model to understand what went wrong and attempt recovery.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 4}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Security and Best Practices for Custom MCP Integrations", "bullets": ["Sanitize inputs: Model outputs must be validated before system execution.", "Principle of least privilege: Scope server execution permissions narrowly.", "Environment variables: Store secrets in standard env files, never hardcoded.", "Continuous integration: Run static analyzers and tests on every config change."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Secure MCP Execution Pipeline", "steps": ["Validate Input Schema", "Check Execution Permissions", "Execute Command", "Sanitize & Format Output"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "As you build custom MCP tools, security and maintainability must be top priorities. Because LLMs generate arguments dynamically, never pass raw input strings directly into system shell commands without strict sanitization and schema checking. Store API keys and credentials in environment variables referenced by your MCP configuration file rather than hardcoding them. Finally, include configuration schema linting in your standard CI pipeline so configuration mistakes never reach main branches.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "summary", "title": "Lecture Summary: Custom Tool Integrations with MCP", "bullets": ["MCP standardizes communication between agent hosts and custom external tools.", "Workspace configuration requires valid JSON adhering strictly to host schemas.", "Tool implementations rely on JSON Schema input contracts and safe error handling.", "Passing integration tests and eliminating static analysis warnings ensures reliability."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "To summarize today's lecture: MCP provides a clean, standardized architecture for extending AI coding agents with custom tools. We learned how host, client, and server components interact, how to configure workspace servers, and how to write tool handlers that handle errors safely. Remember that all public tests must pass and static analysis tools must report zero warnings for workspace integrations. Next week, we will expand on this foundation with hands-on reinforcement labs.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 5}]}

{"lecture_id":"W04.S1","title":"Custom Tool Integrations and Use-Case Specifications","slides":[{"kind":"concept","title":"From Agent Prompts to External Tools","bullets":["An agent can use tools only when their interfaces and permissions are defined.","A tool integration connects an agent workflow to an external capability.","Configuration must define discovery, invocation, results, and failure handling.","Integration tests verify that the agent can call tools correctly."],"figure":{"kind":"flow","caption":"A basic agent-to-tool workflow","steps":["User request","Agent selects tool","Tool executes","Result returns to agent"],"code":"","language":"","rows":[]},"quiz":null,"notes":"Start from the previous class: students already know how coding agents use context and prompts. Today we turn that understanding into a repeatable tool integration. Emphasize that a tool is not merely a function call; its contract also determines what the agent may do, what arguments are valid, and how errors return. The practical goal is a configured integration that passes public tests without analyser warnings. The second half of the lecture connects this operational work to clear use cases and boundaries.","los":["CO1.LO2"],"sources":[],"minutes":4},{"kind":"concept","title":"Model Context Protocol as an Interface Standard","bullets":["Model Context Protocol defines a common way for applications to expose tools.","Clients discover server-provided capabilities through a shared protocol.","Servers describe tools with structured metadata and input schemas.","Clients invoke tools and receive structured results or errors."],"figure":{"kind":"table","caption":"Core roles in a model context protocol integration","steps":[],"code":"","language":"","rows":[["Role","Responsibility","Example"],["Client","Connects to servers and uses capabilities","Coding agent"],["Server","Exposes tools and handles requests","Repository service"],["Tool","Performs one bounded operation","Search files"],["Result","Returns output or a structured error","Search matches"]]},"quiz":null,"notes":"Introduce Model Context Protocol, or MCP, as an interface standard rather than a particular tool implementation. The important separation is between the client, which consumes capabilities, and the server, which exposes them. A tool has metadata and a schema so the client can understand how to call it. This structured contract reduces the need for bespoke instructions for every tool. Ask students to identify which side their coding environment would occupy.","los":["CO1.LO2"],"sources":["C1"],"minutes":5},{"kind":"concept","title":"Anatomy of a Tool Contract","bullets":["Tool metadata identifies the operation and explains its purpose.","Input schemas define required fields, types, and permitted values.","Output schemas make successful results predictable for the agent.","Error behavior should distinguish invalid requests from failed operations."],"figure":{"kind":"code","caption":"Illustrative tool definition","steps":[],"code":"{
  "name": "search_files",
  "description": "Search project files by text",
  "inputSchema": {
    "type": "object",
