}","language":"json","rows":[]},"quiz":null,"notes":"Walk through the example field by field. The name is the stable identifier, while the description helps the agent decide when the tool is relevant. The input schema prevents ambiguous calls by stating what data is required. Although the example does not show an output schema, production integrations should document the result shape as well. Point out that descriptions should state purpose and limits, not embed hidden procedures or unrelated instructions.","los":["CO1.LO2"],"sources":["C1"],"minutes":5},{"kind":"concept","title":"Configure the Integration Boundary","bullets":["Record the server command, transport, and required environment variables.","Keep secrets outside configuration files and source control.","Define least-privilege access for each exposed operation.","Separate development credentials from shared or production credentials."],"figure":{"kind":"flow","caption":"Configuration boundary for a local tool server","steps":["Environment variables","Server command","Transport settings","Tool registry"],"code":"","language":"","rows":[]},"quiz":null,"notes":"Translate protocol concepts into configuration decisions. Students should know how the server is launched, how the client reaches it, and which environment variables it needs. Secrets belong in the environment or an approved secret store, never in committed configuration. Permissions should match the tool's actual purpose: a read-only search tool does not need write access. This boundary also supports later requirements work because capabilities and exclusions become explicit.","los":["CO1.LO2"],"sources":[],"minutes":5},{"kind":"worked_problem","title":"Configure a Read-Only Search Tool","bullets":["Create a server that exposes one file-search operation.","Declare a required query string and a bounded result shape.","Launch the server with a local transport and required variables.","Register the server in the agent workspace configuration."],"figure":{"kind":"code","caption":"Illustrative workspace configuration","steps":[],"code":"{
  "mcpServers": {
    "project-search": {
      "command": "python",
      "args": ["tools/search_server.py"],
      "env": {
        "PROJECT_ROOT": "${PROJECT_ROOT}"
      }
    }
  }
}","language":"json","rows":[]},"quiz":null,"notes":"Model the configuration workflow without claiming that every client uses this exact file format. The server command starts the tool process, arguments identify the implementation, and environment values provide runtime context. The placeholder for the project root avoids hard-coding a machine-specific path. Students should check that the server starts independently, the client discovers the tool, and the tool cannot search outside its permitted root. The next slide turns those checks into tests.","los":["CO1.LO2"],"sources":[],"minutes":6},{"kind":"worked_problem","title":"Test Discovery, Invocation, and Failure","bullets":["Test that the client lists the expected tool and metadata.","Invoke the tool with valid arguments and verify the result.","Send missing or invalid arguments and verify structured errors.","Run the configured static analyser and resolve every warning."],"figure":{"kind":"table","caption":"Integration test cases","steps":[],"code":"","language":"","rows":[["Case","Action","Expected result"],["Discovery","List tools","Search tool appears"],["Success","Search for a known term","Matching file returned"],["Invalid input","Omit required query","Validation error"],["Quality","Run static analyser","No warnings"]]},"quiz":null,"notes":"Treat the public tests as executable acceptance criteria. Discovery confirms that the client and server agree on the exposed capability. A success case checks both the result and its structure. Negative cases ensure invalid calls fail clearly rather than producing misleading output. Finally, run the same static analyser used by the project and remove all warnings, including unused configuration and schema issues. A passing test suite alone does not satisfy the stated quality condition.","los":["CO1.LO2"],"sources":[],"minutes":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Which Configuration Is Safest?","bullets":[],"figure":{"kind":"none"},"quiz":{"question":"A search tool needs a project path and an API token. Which configuration is safest?","options":["Store both values directly in the committed configuration file","Pass the path as an environment value and keep the token in a secret store","Include the token in the tool description sent to the model","Print the token in server logs for easier debugging"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The path can be supplied through environment configuration, while the token should remain in an approved secret store. Committing the token exposes it, and putting it in the description or logs also risks disclosure. The first option is unsafe because it commits a credential.","notes":"Use this question to check whether students distinguish ordinary configuration from secrets. Ask them to explain why a tool description is not a safe place for credentials. The correct option separates a non-secret path from a protected token while preserving a reproducible configuration. Connect the answer to least privilege: exposing only needed values reduces the impact of mistakes.","los":["CO1.LO2"],"sources":[],"minutes":3}, 	"summary":null}]}

