ure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Introduce the three pillars students will use to turn vague product text into testable specifications. Note that the course follows a lightweight IEEE 830 profile suited for agile teams.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Identifying actors, goals, and system boundaries", "bullets": ["Actors = external entities interacting with system", "Goals = observable outcomes actors want", "Boundary separates system from environment"], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Explain the classic use‑case modelling steps. Use a simple e‑commerce example: Customer (actor) wants to place order (goal); payment gateway lies outside the boundary.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Guidelines: IEEE 830, user stories, acceptance criteria", "bullets": ["IEEE 830: functional, non‑functional, interfaces", "User story format: As a <role> I want <goal> so that <benefit>", "Acceptance criteria: Given/When/Then scenarios"], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Contrast the formal IEEE structure with the agile user‑story template. Show how acceptance criteria bridge both worlds and become automated tests in spec‑driven development.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "example", "title": "From product description to use cases", "bullets": ["Original: "Users can track shipments"", "Derived use cases: Track Shipment, Notify Delay"], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Mapping description phrases to use‑case elements", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Description phrase", "Actor", "Goal", "Use case"], [""track shipments"", "Customer", "See real‑time status", "Track Shipment"], [""notify delays"", "System", "Alert on ETA change", "Notify Delay"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Show a side‑by‑side table that breaks a single sentence into two distinct use cases. Highlight how nouns become actors and verbs become goals.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "Outlining use cases for a sample app", "bullets": ["1. Read the one‑paragraph product blurb", "2. List all nouns → candidate actors", "3. List verbs → candidate goals", "4. Draw system boundary", "5. Write user stories + acceptance criteria"], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Step‑by‑step use‑case extraction", "steps": ["Read blurb", "Extract nouns", "Extract verbs", "Define boundary", "Write stories"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Walk through a fictitious "Meal Planner" description live. Produce three user stories with Given/When/Then criteria. This models the skill assessed in Assignment 1.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 8}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Use case identification quiz", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "In a use‑case model, what defines the system boundary?", "options": ["The set of all actors", "The list of functional requirements", "The separation between system responsibilities and external entities", "The acceptance criteria for each story"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The boundary marks what the system controls versus what external actors or services control. Actors lie outside; functional requirements and acceptance criteria live inside."}, "notes": "Check that students can distinguish boundary from actors and requirements. The most tempting distractor is "The set of all actors" because actors are closely linked to boundaries.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 4}, {"kind": "activity", "title": "Hands‑on: MCP config & use‑case drafting", "bullets": ["Pair up: 