{"lecture_id": "W04.S1", "title": "Custom Tool Integrations using Model Context Protocols & Use Case Standards", "slides": [{"kind": "concept", "title": "Model Context Protocol for Agent Tool Integrations", "bullets": ["Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how AI agents communicate with external development tools and servers.", "Context servers expose tools, resources, and prompt templates through a standardized JSON-RPC schema.", "Standardized interfaces decouple model capabilities from specific workspace implementations."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "MCP Architecture Flow", "steps": ["AI Agent Workspace", "MCP Protocol Interface", "Tool Server", "External API/DB"]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Welcome back. Today we cover custom tool integrations using the Model Context Protocol (MCP) and requirements specification. MCP provides an open standard for connecting AI agent workspaces to local or remote tools safely and deterministically. Rather than writing ad-hoc wrappers for every tool, MCP standardizes how tools expose their capabilities, arguments, and return types.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Architecture of MCP Tool Servers", "bullets": ["MCP architecture separates the host client from isolated server tools.", "Tools are registered with strict JSON Schema parameter definitions for model validation.", "Transport options include stdio for local tools and Server-Sent Events (SSE) for remote services."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "MCP System Components", "rows": [["MCP Client", "Workspace host requesting actions", "VS Code Agent Extension"], ["MCP Server", "Exposes executable tools & data", "Custom Git/Database Server"], ["Tool Definition", "JSON schema defining input parameters", "run_linter(path, rules)"], ["Transport Layer", "Communication channel", "stdio / HTTP-SSE"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "The architecture relies on a client-server structure. The client is typically the workspace IDE or agent runtime. The tool server executes functions locally or remotely. Defining strict JSON schemas for inputs ensures that LLM agent function calls can be validated prior to execution.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "Implementing an MCP Tool Server", "bullets": ["Define explicit input/output schemas using typed function signatures.", "Implement error handling directly in the tool handler to avoid runtime crashes.", "Return formatted strings or JSON structures that agents can interpret directly."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Python FastMCP Tool Implementation", "code": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP

