ation guidelines for use cases. Development projects often start with unstructured text from product owners. Our task is to establish clear system boundaries and extract well-defined use cases.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Defining Actors and External Interfaces", "bullets": ["Actors must always be external entities (human users or external systems) interacting with the system.", "The software system being designed is NEVER an actor itself.", "Primary actors trigger use cases; secondary actors assist in completing them."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Actor Identification Matrix", "rows": [["Primary Actor", "User initiating interaction to achieve a goal", "Store Customer"], ["Secondary Actor", "External system or role providing services", "Payment Gateway"], ["System Boundary", "Limit dividing internal logic from actors", "E-commerce API"], ["System Administrator", "Actor managing configuration and access", "System Administrator"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "A critical rule in system modeling: actors are external entities that interact with your system. Humans like 'Customer' or external systems like 'Payment Gateway' or operational actors like 'System Administrator' are actors. The system under design is inside the boundary, so it can never be an actor.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Standard Specification Guidelines for Use Cases", "bullets": ["Standard use case templates ensure consistency across development teams.", "Preconditions state necessary requirements before execution can begin.", "Main success scenarios document step-by-step user interactions and system responses.", "Postconditions define system state changes after successful completion."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Use Case Specification Anatomy", "steps": ["Name & Goal", "Actors", "Preconditions", "Main Success Scenario", "Postconditions"]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Following a standardized specification format keeps requirements unambiguous. Every short use case should capture the goal, external actors, preconditions, step-by-step interaction flow, and postconditions.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "example", "title": "Analyzing an Unstructured Product Description", "bullets": ["Read unstructured descriptions to isolate actors, actions, and integrations.", "Map human users to primary actors and third-party APIs to secondary actors.", "Group related actions under concise, verb-noun use case titles."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Requirement Extraction Mapping", "rows": [["'Users sign in and schedule package deliveries.'", "Customer schedules delivery", "Use Case"], ["'Deliveries are dispatched via third-party couriers.'", "Carrier", "Secondary Actor"], ["'Admins monitor system status and manage users.'", "System Administrator", "Primary Actor"], ["'Payment processed via Stripe integration.'", "Payment Gateway", "Secondary Actor"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In this example, we break down a product statement for a logistics platform. Notice how human roles like 'Admins' map to System Administrator, third-party services like couriers map to 'Carrier', and actions become defined use cases.", "los": ["CO2.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 8}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "From Product Description to Use Cases", "bullets": ["Primary actor (System Administrator) initiates the dispatch workflow.", "External partner (Carrier) acts as a secondary actor providing routing data.", "Note: The internal software system coordinates actions but is not listed as an actor.", "Preconditions and postconditions explicitly lock down state transition criteria."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Structured Use Case Spec in YAML", "code": "use_case: Dispatch Package Delivery
primary_actor: System Administrator
