{"lecture_id": "W07.S2", "title": "Iterative Development and Loop Engineering", "slides": [{"kind": "concept", "title": "What Is Iterative Loop Engineering in AI Development?", "bullets": ["Iterative loop engineering drives autonomous agent code creation through feedback loops.", "Agents generate code, execute tests, evaluate output, and refine solutions repeatedly.", "Tracking agent behavior requires inspecting execution logs across every iteration cycle."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Iterative agent execution loop", "steps": ["Prompt Dispatch", "Code Generation", "Test Execution", "Log Feedback & Refine"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Iterative development with AI agents shifts programming from single-shot prompt engineering to managing structured execution loops. In an iterative loop, the coding agent continuously generates source code, executes tests or static analysis tools, inspects the failure output, and modifies its approach. Understanding this loop requires software engineers to trace state updates and control flow through agent log artifacts, ensuring that iterations converge toward correct code rather than infinite repetition or divergent bugs.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C4", "C6"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "The Structural Components of Agent Execution Loops", "bullets": ["Observation: Agent receives test logs, compiler errors, or tool outputs.", "Thought: Model reasons about the failure cause and selects an action.", "Action: Model issues tool calls such as file edits or test commands.", "Environment State: Execution environment updates state and returns new logs."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Four-phase agent loop interaction", "steps": ["Observation", "Reasoning", "Action Execution", "Environment State Update"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Coding agents operate using an action-observation loop commonly structured around reasoning frameworks like ReAct. Each iteration begins with an observation of the workspace state, followed by internal reasoning where the LLM plans its next edit or command. The agent then executes a tool call, modifying files or running test scripts. Observing how control flows across these four phases in execution logs allows developers to pinpoint exactly where an agent strays off course during complex tasks.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C6"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Log Artifacts as Execution History Records", "bullets": ["Logs capture timestamped records of prompts, model calls, and environmental responses.", "Structured logs format tool invocations, shell commands, and stdout/stderr streams.", "Unstructured trace files contain full raw context windows sent back and forth.", "Tracing logs enables post-mortem analysis of agent reasoning and failure trajectories."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Log artifacts produced during iterative agent tasks serve as the primary source of truth for software developers. Unlike standard application logs, agent logs contain interleaved streams of LLM prompts, model responses, system tool invocations, and workspace environmental feedback like compiler outputs. By systematically analyzing these log artifacts, developers can trace every decision point, verify whether feedback was interpreted correctly, and diagnose systemic failure patterns across long-running development loops.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C3", "C6"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Identifying Control-Flow Steps in Execution Tracing", "bullets": ["Prompt dispatch marks the start of a logical reasoning step.", "Tool invocation indicates a transition from reasoning to environment action.", "Shell execution records system-level tool results and exit codes.", "State evaluation determines whether to continue looping or terminate."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Control-flow step classification in agent logs", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Step Type", "Log Indicator", "Information Captured"], ["Prompt Dispatch", "system_prompt / user_input", "Context window & instructions"], ["Tool Invoc", "call_tool / function_call", "Arguments & file targets"], ["Shell Output", "stdout / stderr / exit_code", "Test results & error traces"], ["Evaluation", "loop_status / terminate", "Convergence state"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Tracing execution logs requires categorizing log events into distinct control-flow steps. A typical trace includes prompt dispatch steps, tool calls like file reading or patch application, shell execution steps with process status codes, and loop condition evaluations. Recognizing these steps within multi-megabyte log files allows engineers to construct a step-by-step state machine of the agent's behavior, showing how inputs led to actions and how test outcomes modified subsequent prompts.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C2", "C6"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Check Point: Identifying Agent Loop Components", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "When tracing an agent log artifact, which log event signals a transition from internal reasoning to active workspace modification?", "options": ["The raw system prompt dispatch sent to the model context window.", "A tool invocation event executing a patch or write_file command.", "A stdout stream capture containing unit test assertion results.", "The final model response token indicating execution completion."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Option 1 is correct because a tool invocation event (e.g., executing a patch or writing a file) represents the exact boundary where the agent acts upon the environment. Option 0 is model input, Option 2 is environment feedback (observation), and Option 3 represents loop completion rather than an active workspace modification step."}, "notes": "This check tests your understanding of execution control flow in agent logs. Identifying the distinction between model prompt dispatches, tool invocations, and environment feedback is essential when reading structured traces to determine when and how an agent modifies project state.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C6"], "minutes": 4}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Log Formatting and Schema Standards", "bullets": ["JSON-Lines and OpenTelemetry provide structured formats for agent trace events.", "Key fields include trace_id, span_id, timestamp, step_type, and payload.", "Tool execution payloads record input arguments, execution duration, and exit status.", "Standardized schemas allow automated log parsing and visual trace representation."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Structured JSON-Lines event entry from an agent execution trace", "steps": [], "code": "{
