al test failure log in iteration 1.", "Step 2: Record file edit action in iteration 2 that causes new regression.", "Step 3: Observe revert and re-application of initial bug in iteration 3.", "Step 4: Map control-flow loop cycles to document agent oscillation."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Oscillating loop trace progression", "steps": ["Test Failure A", "Edit Function 1", "Test Failure B", "Revert Function 1", "Return to Failure A"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In this worked problem, we trace a log artifact where an agent becomes stuck in an oscillating loop. Step 1 records the initial failure. In step 2, the agent modifies function A, fixing bug 1 but breaking bug 2. In step 3, seeing bug 2 fail, the agent reverts function A and breaks bug 1 again. By mapping each tool invocation and test exit code chronologically in our trace sheet, we document how missing context in the prompt causes endless cycling between two failure states.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C3", "C6"], "minutes": 8}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Complexity Backpressure and Failure Modes in Traces", "bullets": ["Complexity backpressure occurs when context accumulation degrades model reasoning.", "Repeated patch failures introduce noise and context window bloat in traces.", "Oscillating loops, premature termination, and hallucinated tool arguments appear in logs.", "Tracing logs exposes when context limits degrade patch quality."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "As agent loops extend over many iterations, context windows accumulate historical failure logs, standard error outputs, and discarded diffs. Research shows this causes 'complexity backpressure,' where model reasoning degrades due to context saturation. In execution logs, backpressure manifests as repeated patch retries, hallucinated file paths, or premature agent termination. Tracing these indicators helps engineers determine when to reset agent state rather than letting the loop run continuously.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C3"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Check Point: Analyzing Loop Failure Indicators", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "While inspecting an execution log trace, you notice an agent executing identical patch tools and test runs across four consecutive iterations with no code changes. What does this log pattern signify?", "options": ["Successful convergence toward an optimal patch solution.", "An unhandled error state causing an infinite non-convergent loop.", "Normal behavior caused by stochastic variation in model output.", "External network rate limiting forcing tool command replay."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Option 1 is correct because executing identical actions without modifying environment state indicates the agent is trapped in an infinite non-convergent loop. Option 0 is wrong because the code is not changing or converging. Option 2 misattributes loop stagnation to normal variation. Option 3 is incorrect because tool calls are explicit agent decisions, not replayed network packets."}, "notes": "This question tests your ability to diagnose failure modes directly from trace observations. Recognizing when an agent repeats actions without modifying state allows you to catch infinite loops early and intervene.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C3", "C6"], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "activity", "title": "Hands-on Activity: Trace Construction from Raw Logs", "bullets": ["Download the provided raw log artifact containing 20 execution step events.", "Parse log JSON entries and map out the agent's control-flow trajectory.", "Identify all tool calls, test outcomes, and iteration boundaries in your trace table.", "Highlight the exact log step where control flow diverged from expected logic."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In this hands-on activity, you will work in pairs using student development machines. You are given a raw JSON-lines log artifact from an agent attempting a refactoring task. Your goal is to construct a complete chronological trace map, listing every control-flow step, tool invocation, test result, and state transition. Identify the exact step where the agent misinterpreted a compiler error and record your findings in the lab worksheet.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C6"], "minutes": 12}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Log-Informed Loop Engineering and Query Tuning", "bullets": ["Traces reveal exact prompts and context states that triggered agent errors.", "Query tuning modifies prompt templates based on observed log failure points.", "Context pruning truncates historical tool outputs to alleviate backpressure.", "Model Context Protocol (MCP) enables standardized context injection in loops."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Trace-driven loop tuning workflow", "steps": ["Trace Log Analysis", "Identify Context Bloat", "Tune MCP Prompt", "Re-run Agent Loop"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Tracing logs is not just a diagnostic tool—it directly informs query tuning and loop refinement. By analyzing execution traces, engineers discover where prompts were ambiguous or where tool output flooded the context window. Using frameworks like the Model Context Protocol (MCP), developers can dynamically prune irrelevant history or adjust system prompts, ensuring subsequent iterations maintain lean context and precise instruction flow.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C5"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Integrating Automated Tracing into Software Pipelines", "bullets": ["Automated log collectors capture telemetry across multi-agent software tasks.", "Dashboard visualizations render state transitions, tool latency, and token costs.", "Guardrail checks monitor log traces in real time to break infinite loops.", "Structured log retention enables continuous evaluation of agent capabilities."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Automated log telemetry and guardrail pipeline", "steps": ["Agent Execution", "Telemetry Collector", "Real-Time Guardrail", "Dashboard / Intervene"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In modern software factories, log tracing is integrated directly into CI/CD pipelines and developer tooling. Telemetry collectors stream log events in real time, feeding dashboards that visualize state transitions, token consumption, and execution bottlenecks. Furthermore, automated guardrails monitor traces live: if a log shows three identical failing iterations, the execution loop is automatically halted, saving API costs and preventing corrupted git commits.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C4", "C6"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "summary", "title": "Summary: Tracing Agent Iterative Development Loops", "bullets": ["Iterative loop engineering relies on observing and directing agent execution loops.", "Tracing log artifacts requires tracking prompt dispatch, tool calls, and state changes.", "Log patterns expose failure modes like complexity backpressure and infinite loops.", "Continuous trace analysis enables targeted query tuning and pipeline guardrails."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "To summarize today's lecture, mastering iterative development with AI coding agents requires deep visibility into execution logs. By tracing every control-flow step across log artifacts, you can diagnose where agents fail, detect complexity backpressure early, and apply query tuning to fix loop behavior. Next week, we will expand on these trace artifacts to analyze software factory outputs and multi-agent coordination records.", "los": ["CO3.LO1"], "sources": ["C3", "C4", "C6"], "minutes": 5