dance"], ["Ensemble", "Aggregated", "High-stakes decisions"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Learned evaluators replace or augment human judges at scale. AlphaCode (C1) used a combination of execution testing and clustering to select submissions. Reward models trained on human preferences can score functional correctness without running code. Critique agents explain failures in natural language, enabling automated repair loops. All require careful calibration against ground truth.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C1", "C6"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Evaluation metrics for code generation", "bullets": ["Pass@k: fraction of problems solved in k attempts", "Syntax validity rate: compiles without errors", "Functional correctness: passes hidden test suites", "Style and maintainability: lint scores, complexity"], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Key metrics from AlphaCode (C1)", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Metric", "Definition", "AlphaCode result"], ["Pass@1", "First attempt correct", "~30% on CodeContests"], ["Pass@10", "Any of 10 attempts correct", "~50%"], ["Pass@100", "Any of 100 attempts correct", "~70%"], ["Valid syntax", "Compiles/runs", ">99%"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "AlphaCode (C1) established Pass@k as the standard metric for competitive programming. Pass@1 measures single-shot reliability; Pass@10 and Pass@100 measure potential with sampling. Syntax validity is near-perfect for modern LLMs. Functional correctness on hidden tests is the true measure. Style metrics (cyclomatic complexity, nesting) correlate with maintainability.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Specification-driven evaluation (C2)", "bullets": ["Specs as executable acceptance criteria", "Pre/post conditions checked automatically", "Invariant verification across state changes", "Traceability: each test maps to a spec clause"], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Spec as test oracle", "steps":