// Auto-generated test checks all clauses", "language": "text", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Specification-driven development (C2) elevates specs to first-class artifacts. Each spec clause becomes a test oracle. Preconditions validate inputs; postconditions validate outputs; invariants validate state consistency. This creates traceability: a failing test points directly to a violated spec clause, enabling targeted repair.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C2"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Complexity backpressure as quality gate (C3)", "bullets": ["Measure: cyclomatic complexity, nesting, token count", "Thresholds trigger regeneration or simplification", "Prevents over-engineered, brittle solutions", "Correlates with bug density in generated code"], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Backpressure loop", "steps": ["Generate", "Measure complexity", "Exceeds threshold?", "Regenerate simpler"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Complexity backpressure (C3) treats code complexity as a proxy for reliability. When generated code exceeds complexity thresholds, the agent is prompted to simplify. This reduces over-fitting to the prompt and produces more maintainable code. Empirical studies show complexity correlates with bug density even in AI-generated code.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C3"], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Autonomous agents for test generation and repair (C6)", "bullets": ["Agents write tests from specifications", "Self-healing: test failure triggers code repair", "Test minimization: reduce flaky or redundant tests", "Continuous evaluation in CI/CD pipelines"], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Agent roles in testing", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Role", "Action", "Input"], ["Test writer", "Generate test cases", "Spec, code"], ["Repair agent", "Fix code to pass tests", "Failure trace"], ["Critic", "Review test quality", "Test suite"], ["Orchestrator", "Run pipeline", "Commit"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Autonomous coding agents (C6) close the loop: they generate tests from specs, run them, and repair code on failure. The critic agent evaluates test quality (coverage, flakiness, redundancy). The orchestrator manages the pipeline in CI/CD. This creates a self-improving system where test quality and code quality co-evolve.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C6"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "Worked example: comparing two testing pipelines", "bullets": ["Pipeline A: static + dynamic + LLM judge", "Pipeline B: spec-driven + backpressure + human review", "Scenario: generate REST API from OpenAPI spec", "Compare on: false negative rate, latency, cost"], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Pipeline comparison for REST API generation", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Criterion", "Pipeline A", "Pipeline B"], ["False negatives", "Low (execution catches)", "Medium (spec gaps)"], ["Latency", "High (sandbox + LLM)", "Medium (static fast)"], ["Cost per PR", "$0.50 (LLM calls)", "$0.10 (mostly static)"], ["Spec coverage", "Implicit", "Explicit traceability"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Pipeline A uses execution-heavy validation with an LLM judge for semantic checks. It catches more runtime errors but is slower and costlier. Pipeline B relies on specification-driven static checks and complexity backpressure, with human review only for high-risk changes. It is faster and cheaper but may miss errors not covered by the spec. The choice depends on reliability requirements: safety-critical systems favor Pipeline A; internal tools may prefer Pipeline B.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C2", "C3", "C6"], "minutes": 8}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Check: Which metric measures single-shot reliability?", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "Which evaluation metric best measures the single-shot reliability of a code generation model?", "options": ["Pass@1", "Pass@10", "Pass@100", "Syntax validity rate"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Pass@1 measures the fraction of problems solved on the first attempt, directly reflecting single-shot reliability. Pass@10 and Pass@100 measure potential with sampling. Syntax validity only checks compilation, not functional correctness."}, "notes": "Pass@k metrics come from AlphaCode (C1). Pass@1 is the strictest and most relevant for production deployments where sampling multiple times is impractical.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 3}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Check: What does complexity backpressure prevent?", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "What is the primary purpose of complexity backpressure in AI coding agents?", "options": ["Reduce token usage to save cost", "Prevent over-engineered, brittle solutions", "Increase test coverage automatically", "Speed up code generation latency"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Complexity backpressure (C3) rejects overly complex generated code and forces regeneration of simpler solutions. This prevents over-engineering and correlates with lower bug density. It does not directly reduce token cost, increase coverage, or speed up generation."}, "notes": "C3 shows that complexity metrics (cyclomatic, nesting, tokens) act as quality gates. High complexity in generated code correlates with higher bug rates, so backpressure improves reliability.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C3"], "minutes": 3}, {"kind": "summary", "title": "Summary: Choosing testing mechanisms for AI-native systems", "bullets": ["Layer mechanisms: static → dynamic → learned → human", "Match metrics to reliability requirements: Pass@1 for production, Pass@k for exploration", "Specification-driven testing enables traceability and automated oracles", "Complexity backpressure is a low-cost quality gate", "Autonomous agents close the generate-test-repair loop"], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "No single mechanism suffices. Effective pipelines combine static analysis (fast, cheap), dynamic execution (high confidence), learned evaluators (semantic scale), and human review (ground truth). Metrics must align with the reliability requirement: safety-critical code demands Pass@1 with execution; exploratory tasks can use Pass@k. Specification-driven approaches (C2) and complexity backpressure (C3) provide structure and guardrails. Autonomous agents (C6) automate the loop.", "los": ["CO4.LO1"], "sources": ["C1", "C2", "C3", "C6"], "minutes": 5}]}

