oint?", "options": ["Average CPU utilization of the model server", "p99 end-to-end latency including token generation", "Number of GPU instances running", "Training loss of the underlying model"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "p99 end-to-end latency directly measures the worst-case user experience, including token streaming delays. CPU utilization and GPU count are infrastructure metrics, not user-facing. Training loss is a model development metric, not a deployment metric."}, "notes": "Quick check to ensure students distinguish user-facing latency metrics from infrastructure and model metrics. The most tempting wrong answer is average CPU utilization because it's commonly monitored but doesn't reflect tail latency.", "los": ["CO4.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Check: Reliability Metric Relationships", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "If a service has an SLO of 99.9% availability over 30 days, what is its error budget in minutes?", "options": ["43.2 minutes", "4.32 minutes", "432 minutes", "0.432 minutes"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "30 days = 43,200 minutes. 0.1% of 43,200 = 43.2 minutes. The error budget is the allowed downtime. 4.32 minutes would be for 99.99% (four nines). 432 minutes is 1% (two nines). 0.432 minutes is 0.001% (five nines)."}, "notes": "Tests understanding of error budget calculation. Common mistake: confusing percentage with decimal (0.1% vs 0.1). Also confusing days to minutes conversion.", "los": ["CO4.LO3"], "sources": [], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Best Practices for Continuous Metric Evaluation", "bullets": ["Automate SLO dashboards and alerting on error budget burn rate", "Include model quality SLIs (correctness, hallucination rate)", "Run synthetic canary requests every minute", "Correlate deployments with metric shifts using change events", "Review and adjust SLOs quarterly with stakeho